DONE (2026-08-14): aisand prune no longer touches memory volumes. It
removes images only, which are wholly derivable from the Dockerfile
plus the config files. Memory deletion moved to an explicit "aisand
forget [PATH...]", and "aisand memory" lists volumes with project,
size, and date. No --all on forget: it is the one irreversible bulk
operation here and withholding the verb beats a confirmation prompt.
This also retires the orphaned-aisand-pkgs-image note that used to
live here — prune is now safe to run freely, so prune IS the gc.

DONE (2026-08-08): pre-allowing tool permissions is moot — aisand now
passes --dangerously-skip-permissions unconditionally, since the
container is the trust boundary. No settings.json allow-list needed.

WON'T DO (2026-08-08): multi-stage build to drop nodejs/npm from the
runtime image. It would work — they are build-time only; the npm
package ships bin/claude.exe, a native ELF binary that links only
against libc/libm/libpthread/librt/libdl and runs with node off PATH
(verified against image aisand, claude 2.1.220) — and would save
~195 MB. Rejected because minimizing the base image is not a goal;
convenience of installing claude via npm is worth the space. Keep
nodejs/npm as long as npm is the install method. The rule for this
image is "nothing that serves no purpose", not "as small as possible".

investigate whether claude update can be made to work for the
non-root container user (e.g., installing claude into $HOME instead
of system-wide). Currently users must aisand rebuild.

consider whether to mount more of the host's claude config than just
the API key approval list — e.g., model preferences, custom commands.

handle host with no ~/.gitconfig: warn the user that git commit will
fail until they configure one (on the host or inside the container).

can the claude code prompt itself be modified to show when it is in the sandbox?

DONE (2026-08-14): pip_packages, implemented as sketched — a venv at
/opt/venv built into the derived image and prepended to PATH, so the
install cost is paid once per package-set change. The open question
resolved to ONE hash over both lists, since the image is a function of
the pair. Dead ends recorded here for future-me: system pip is blocked
by PEP 668 (/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED); pip install
--user does not survive a run because $HOME is tmpfs; a venv inside
the repo persists but puts build artifacts in the source tree. Do NOT
reach for --break-system-packages. Deliberately not
--system-site-packages either — see IMPLEMENTATION.md for why.

DONE (2026-08-14): pip_packages verified against a real build in
kaleidoscope (build123d, trimesh, numpy, pillow). Image
aisand-pkgs-00a76ba863e6, 3.25 GB against a 1.69 GB base; /opt/venv is
802 MB. cadquery-ocp resolves to the novtk wheel
(cp311/manylinux_2_31, 67.6 MB) so nothing builds from source — the
base image's Python 3.11 and glibc 2.36 are what make that wheel
eligible, worth remembering before any base-image bump. The venv gets
numpy 2.4.6, which was the whole point: bookworm apt caps at 1.24.2.
tests/smoke_cad.py in kaleidoscope passes; its reported volume matches
the analytic value to three decimals, so the geometry is right and not
merely non-crashing.

kaleidoscope's X/GL list is larger than OCP actually needs. ldd on the
one OCP .so shows only libGL.so.1 and libX11.so.6 (plus libGLX and
libGLdispatch pulled in by libGL); fontconfig and freetype are
VENDORED into the wheel (libfontconfig-ebabad56.so.1.12.0), so the apt
copies go unused. Unreferenced: libglu1-mesa, libxext6, libxrender1,
libxi6, libsm6, libice6, libfontconfig1, libfreetype6. Trimming buys
little — apt reported gnuplot-nox, libfontconfig1, libfreetype6,
libx11-6, libxext6 and libxrender1 as already present in the base, so
only libglu1-mesa, libice6, libsm6 and libxi6 are genuinely extra, and
they are small. The bulk of the apt cost is libgl1's own tree
(libgl1-mesa-dri, libllvm15), and libgl1 is required. Caveat if
trimming anyway: ldd shows direct DT_NEEDED only, so anything dlopened
at runtime would not appear in that list.
